WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:03.200
[ Theme music plays ]

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.234
-Hey, just a short one today.

00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:07.534
I know we're all upset
when they're not longer.

00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:10.801
But we can only correct
the mistakes we make.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:12.000
If we don't make
a ton of mistakes,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.534
we can't do
a long "Corrections,"

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:16.667
because we're not -- I'm not --
ever gonna pad this out.

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.667
Okay?
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:22.100
Because if "Corrections"
is about anything,

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.534
it's about integrity,
and we're never gonna do that.

00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:28.467
I would argue, there's nothing

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:32.567
on TV that I've seen

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.100
that has more integrity
than "Corrections."

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.601
It should be noted,
I don't watch a ton of TV.

00:00:38.601 --> 00:00:41.734
It's just so...

00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:43.667
It's all so woke.

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.634
We talked a lot
about the documents

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.534
that President Biden kept
next to his Corvette.

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:56.634
Did a joke about Ferris Bueller

00:00:56.634 --> 00:00:58.567
stealing his Corvette.

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:00.968
A lot of you said,
Ferris Bueller

00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:03.400
stole a Ferrari, not a Corvette.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:08.400
Sure, but if Cameron's dad
only had a Corvette,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.534
I think Ferris Bueller
would have seen that.

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:12.934
I don't think it was specific
to that kind.

00:01:12.934 --> 00:01:15.334
I mean, would Ferris not have
gotten on the float

00:01:15.334 --> 00:01:17.234
if the songs weren't

00:01:17.234 --> 00:01:19.334
"Danke Schoen"
and "Twist and Shout"?

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:20.934
Look, I get how this happens,
right?

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:25.300
You guys see a mistake,
and you race to your computers.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.434
"Actually, we're already
at our computers."

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:29.367
O-O-Okay!

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:31.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.868 --> 00:01:34.901
But [Sighs] you gotta...

00:01:34.901 --> 00:01:38.000
slow down sometimes.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.067
Okay?

00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:43.067
Life moves pretty fast.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:47.901
And if you don't stop and
look around once in a while...

00:01:47.901 --> 00:01:51.300
[ Laughter ]
...you miss it.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.834
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:53.834 --> 00:01:57.234
Alright, we had a joke --
a California man

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:58.968
recently got out of his car
to take a phone call

00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:02.434
and returned to find the vehicle
had been crushed by a boulder.

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:04.801
I think we have a picture
of the guy.

00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:07.601
Showed a picture of Road Runner.

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:10.334
Joke works, by the way.

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.467
No complaints in the audience.

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:15.033
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:17.067
But then a bunch of you said,
"You know,

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:18.834
wouldn't have been
the Road Runner's car

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:20.667
that got ruined by the boulder.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:25.334
Based on the mythology
of the Coyote and Road Runner,

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:28.167
it probably would have been
the Coyote's car

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:32.367
that got crushed by the boulder,
not Road Runner's.

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:34.501
One person, though,
said something

00:02:34.501 --> 00:02:36.033
that really stuck with me.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:38.901
He made that argument and
said the Coyote's car

00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:41.100
would be destroyed
by the boulder,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.868
and then the Road Runner
would come over

00:02:44.868 --> 00:02:47.868
and say whatever obscenities
they had to meep out.

00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:51.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:53.934
Which I never in my life
considered.

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:56.200
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.367
I thought the Road Runner
was saying, "Meep meep,"

00:02:58.367 --> 00:03:03.801
but now I'm realizing
it was Warner Bros. censoring

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:06.567
whatever obscene thing.

00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:08.567
So, this is what
you would normally see.

00:03:08.567 --> 00:03:10.667
[ Dramatic music plays ]

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:14.834
[ Whistle ]

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:16.467
[ Thud, clatter ]

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:17.901
-Meep meep!

00:03:17.901 --> 00:03:21.567
-And so we went --
We sent our researchers out...

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.834
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.834 --> 00:03:25.734
...to find an uncensored --

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:28.367
again, didn't even know
it was censored --

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:32.968
the uncensored original version
of that same scene.

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:36.534
[ Dramatic music plays ]

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:40.200
[ Whistle ]

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.334
[ Thud, clatter ]

00:03:41.334 --> 00:03:42.834
-Back up!

00:03:42.834 --> 00:03:45.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:53.934
-[ Clears throat ]

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:58.133
We oft make jokes
about how Vladimir Putin

00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:03.000
is prone to defenestrating
his enemies.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.634
And we made a joke about someone

00:04:06.634 --> 00:04:09.501
falling out
a fourth-story window.

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:11.868
Someone wrote that a fall
from a fourth-floor

00:04:11.868 --> 00:04:15.300
wouldn't kill you.
Studies show, eighth floor

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.267
is what you need to be sure.

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:19.067
And I believe that

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:21.067
because our offices
are on the eighth floor.

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:24.167
But when we started our show,
NBC offered us

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:29.968
far more beautiful, sprawling,
modern offices

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:32.534
on the fourth floor.

00:04:32.534 --> 00:04:35.234
And I remember, I didn't
understand why at the time.

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:38.534
I remember Shoemaker saying,
"That's not high enough."

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:41.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.033
I did throw a little shade
last week at people

00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:50.033
for not knowing how to spell
the name Shoemaker,

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:52.033
because it is in the credits
every night.

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:53.367
And then a bunch of you
pointed out,

00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:56.734
"We only watch 'Corrections,'
and there are no credits

00:04:56.734 --> 00:04:58.667
in 'Corrections,'"
which is very fair.

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:01.400
But, you guys, you gotta
watch the show on NBC.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:06.200
Just turn the volume down.
Put it on and go to bed.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.200
That's all that matters.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.501 --> 00:05:13.734
[ Clears throat ]

00:05:13.734 --> 00:05:17.868
MGM lion roar --
looked it up.

00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:20.167
Somebody said it.
Looked it up. It's true.

00:05:20.167 --> 00:05:23.133
Since 1980,

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:25.133
it's actually been a tiger roar,

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:27.934
because tigers have
fiercer roars.

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:30.868
And so this was a decision
that was made by, you know,

00:05:30.868 --> 00:05:33.701
whatever MGM's, I guess,
Mike Witzer is.

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:36.534
Um, so, let's -- You get --

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:38.434
So, here's the roar.
Let's take a listen.

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:43.801
[ Lion roaring ]

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.167
And I, of course --
I'm no expert

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:47.901
on this sort of thing,
but I was told,

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:49.801
if you could slow it down,
you can hear --

00:05:49.801 --> 00:05:51.934
you can tell
that it's not a lion.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:53.367
Let's take a listen.

00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:57.901
-[ Slowed down ] Rowr.
I'm a tiger.

00:05:57.901 --> 00:06:01.300
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.801
-[ Sucks lips ]

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:11.501
Mentioned
a mid-century credenza,

00:06:11.501 --> 00:06:14.734
and, um, a bunch
of furniture heads...

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:17.234
[ Light laughter ]

00:06:17.234 --> 00:06:19.300
Somebody literally said,

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.667
"I don't know
what century that is."

00:06:21.667 --> 00:06:23.334
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.334 --> 00:06:26.300
Look, if you want to sit down
and write something

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.834
about the verisimilitude

00:06:28.834 --> 00:06:32.334
of a credenza's
date of construction,

00:06:32.334 --> 00:06:34.334
you don't have to.

00:06:34.334 --> 00:06:36.501
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.167
Talked about the rewrite we got

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:43.434
on a really good
Amelia Earhart joke.

00:06:43.434 --> 00:06:46.934
And then someone talked about
how they had --

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:49.000
There's a couple
of Amelia Earhart statues.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.000
There's one in North Hollywood,
and somebody wrote

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.901
a very long comment
about their friends --

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:57.901
them and their friends
watching it get built

00:06:57.901 --> 00:07:00.334
and having no idea who it was
while it was getting built.

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:03.767
And now I think
it's also at Purdue University.

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:05.834
But they said it's remarkable

00:07:05.834 --> 00:07:07.667
this is what they went with
for Amelia Earhart.

00:07:07.667 --> 00:07:10.534
So, that's the statue
of Amelia Earhart.

00:07:10.534 --> 00:07:12.801
And I will say,
based on what happened,

00:07:12.801 --> 00:07:16.000
it seems weird to make a statue
where she's holding

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.534
part of the plane that should
definitely be attached.

00:07:18.534 --> 00:07:21.901
[ Laughter ]

00:07:28.534 --> 00:07:30.601
[ Clears throat ]

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.267
"Jokes Seth Can't Tell" --
I've never noticed this.

00:07:37.267 --> 00:07:40.667
The opening graphics,
a piece of tape

00:07:40.667 --> 00:07:43.467
goes over my mouth,
but it is the sound

00:07:43.467 --> 00:07:45.467
of tape getting
pulled off a mouth.

00:07:45.467 --> 00:07:47.701
Take a look.
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:07:47.701 --> 00:07:49.100
♪♪

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.601
[ Tape tearing ]

00:07:53.801 --> 00:07:55.567
We found a solution, though.
Let's take a look.

00:07:55.567 --> 00:07:57.267
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:07:57.267 --> 00:07:59.267
-[ Roars ]

00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:02.734
Tape going on.

00:08:02.734 --> 00:08:06.167
[ Laughter ]

00:08:10.434 --> 00:08:12.000
[ Sucks lips ]

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.300
This next one is a little tough
to talk about.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.133
Um, yesterday's mono.

00:08:17.133 --> 00:08:18.901
[ Inhales deeply ]

00:08:18.901 --> 00:08:21.234
We had this joke
"and finally."

00:08:21.234 --> 00:08:23.467
"And finally," by the way,
when that's in the joke,

00:08:23.467 --> 00:08:26.033
that means it's the closer.

00:08:26.033 --> 00:08:29.734
So that --
there's no joke coming

00:08:29.734 --> 00:08:32.067
to sort of clean it up.

00:08:32.067 --> 00:08:34.100
"And finally, the world's
oldest known person,

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.868
a French nun, has reported died
at the age of 118.

00:08:36.868 --> 00:08:38.767
And I got to say,
I'd feel pretty bad

00:08:38.767 --> 00:08:41.000
if I was the one
who bought her that skateboard."

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.901
[ Light laughter ]

00:08:43.901 --> 00:08:47.601
That joke did not go well.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:47.601 --> 00:08:49.701
And obviously,
it's always tragic --

00:08:49.701 --> 00:08:51.267
To explain ourselves,

00:08:51.267 --> 00:08:54.167
it's always tragic
when someone passes away.

00:08:54.167 --> 00:08:56.868
I would argue,
it's the least tragic

00:08:56.868 --> 00:09:00.267
when that person dies
of natural causes

00:09:00.267 --> 00:09:02.133
at 118 years old.

00:09:02.133 --> 00:09:04.033
[ Light laughter ]

00:09:04.033 --> 00:09:05.834
I mean, the last few years,

00:09:05.834 --> 00:09:08.801
you could even argue
they're just showing off.

00:09:08.801 --> 00:09:11.100
[ Light laughter ]

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.367
But the mistake was, guys,

00:09:13.367 --> 00:09:15.934
that I cannot defend,

00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:18.000
uh, we showed a picture.

00:09:19.901 --> 00:09:22.434
[ Laughter ]
You can't --

00:09:22.434 --> 00:09:24.801
You can't show a picture.

00:09:24.801 --> 00:09:27.400
I mean...

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.667
how in God's name

00:09:29.667 --> 00:09:32.901
did we think a laugh
was forthcoming

00:09:32.901 --> 00:09:35.467
when we put that up...

00:09:35.467 --> 00:09:38.467
[ Laughter ]

00:09:38.467 --> 00:09:42.200
...for the audience to see?

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:45.267
[ Clears throat ]

00:09:45.267 --> 00:09:46.634
[ Sniffles ]

00:09:46.634 --> 00:09:48.868
I'm not gonna say
who wrote that joke,

00:09:48.868 --> 00:09:51.067
but I am gonna say
that when I told it,

00:09:51.067 --> 00:09:54.467
I was...
-♪ Shot down ♪

00:09:54.467 --> 00:10:03.267
[ Singing indistinctly ]

00:10:03.267 --> 00:10:05.601
-Raised Catholic.

00:10:05.601 --> 00:10:08.367
[ Laughter ]

00:10:08.367 --> 00:10:11.434
P.O. box [Clears throat]

00:10:11.434 --> 00:10:13.367
Sometimes it's great.

00:10:13.367 --> 00:10:17.000
Maya knit this for me.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.567
Wrote a very nice letter.

00:10:18.567 --> 00:10:20.501
"Here's a draft
that you can have."

00:10:20.501 --> 00:10:23.634
That's the sort of reason
we have the P.O. box.

00:10:23.634 --> 00:10:27.167
Meaningful gifts

00:10:27.167 --> 00:10:30.000
that can be added

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.100
to our menagerie
of lovely things.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:36.300
This is the kind of thing,
I can't stress enough,

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:38.901
I don't want any more.

00:10:38.901 --> 00:10:40.534
Talked about Mac Tonight.

00:10:40.534 --> 00:10:43.868
We talked about how distressing
I find Mac Tonight.

00:10:43.868 --> 00:10:45.701
This is a piece of original art

00:10:45.701 --> 00:10:49.868
that once I have seen,
I can't unsee this.

00:10:49.868 --> 00:10:52.501
Like, what is this even...

00:10:52.501 --> 00:10:55.934
[ Laughter ]

00:10:55.934 --> 00:10:59.467
What are you trying to say?
Are we the same person?

00:10:59.467 --> 00:11:03.667
Is his evil inside of me?

00:11:03.667 --> 00:11:05.534
[ Laughter ]

00:11:05.534 --> 00:11:07.801
When he goes on a rampage...

00:11:07.801 --> 00:11:09.934
[ Laughter ]

00:11:09.934 --> 00:11:12.834
Is that blood on mine hands?

00:11:12.834 --> 00:11:15.000
[ Light laughter ]

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.100
Do I turn into him...

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.634
unknowing to myself?

00:11:21.667 --> 00:11:23.434
Does the transformation begin

00:11:23.434 --> 00:11:27.367
every time I see half a wolf?

00:11:27.367 --> 00:11:30.400
[ Laughter ]

00:11:31.701 --> 00:11:33.701
That joke's gonna wax on you.

00:11:33.701 --> 00:11:36.701
[ Laughter ]

00:11:39.801 --> 00:11:42.367
Well, [Clears throat]

00:11:42.367 --> 00:11:45.000
like I said,

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.767
it was a short one this week.

00:11:47.767 --> 00:11:50.133
And we do not pad.

00:11:50.133 --> 00:11:55.334
Not now, not ever
do we pad "Corrections."

00:11:55.334 --> 00:11:58.367
So you will see me next week.

00:11:58.367 --> 00:12:01.501
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:12:01.501 --> 00:12:09.634
♪♪

00:12:09.634 --> 00:12:17.801
♪♪

00:12:17.801 --> 00:12:25.934
♪♪

00:12:25.934 --> 00:12:34.067
♪♪

00:12:34.067 --> 00:12:42.200
♪♪

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:50.367
♪♪

00:12:50.367 --> 00:12:58.501
♪♪

00:12:58.501 --> 00:12:59.968
[ Laughter ]

00:12:59.968 --> 00:13:02.300
Don't show the...
-Meep meep.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:03.467
-...picture!

00:13:03.467 --> 00:13:06.467
[ Looney Tunes theme music
plays ]

00:13:06.467 --> 00:13:10.467
♪♪

